WEBVTT

00:00:00.550 --> 00:00:02.185
Lesson 4.2.

00:00:02.185 --> 00:00:03.605
Native staking.

00:00:03.605 --> 00:00:07.180
By the end, you'll understand exactly what staking rewards

00:00:07.180 --> 00:00:10.150
pay for, the real risks underneath them, and why

00:00:10.150 --> 00:00:13.240
staking is never a defence against a falling price.

00:00:14.540 --> 00:00:15.830
Here's the plan.

00:00:15.830 --> 00:00:18.334
What staking rewards actually pay for, and

00:00:18.334 --> 00:00:20.555
the two ways you can participate.

00:00:20.555 --> 00:00:22.839
The real risks underneath a staking

00:00:22.839 --> 00:00:26.375
yield: slashing, unbonding, and validator downtime.

00:00:26.375 --> 00:00:29.104
A worked example showing exactly why staking

00:00:29.104 --> 00:00:32.072
is never protection against a falling price.

00:00:32.072 --> 00:00:33.790
And a checklist for choosing who

00:00:33.790 --> 00:00:35.247
to stake with, and how much.

00:00:36.547 --> 00:00:38.882
What staking rewards actually pay for.

00:00:40.182 --> 00:00:43.977
Here's the mechanism underneath every proof-of-stake network.

00:00:43.977 --> 00:00:47.414
Stakers lock up the network's own asset, specifically

00:00:47.414 --> 00:00:50.342
to help secure the chain, by running software

00:00:50.342 --> 00:00:53.125
that proposes and validates new blocks.

00:00:53.125 --> 00:00:56.789
In return, the network pays stakers rewards, funded by

00:00:56.789 --> 00:00:59.953
a mix of new issuance and transaction fees.

00:00:59.953 --> 00:01:02.911
You can participate in exactly one of two ways:

00:01:02.911 --> 00:01:06.548
run a validator yourself, directly, or delegate your stake

00:01:06.548 --> 00:01:08.828
to someone else who already runs one.

00:01:10.129 --> 00:01:12.701
Here's the actual choice between those two

00:01:12.701 --> 00:01:15.034
ways to participate, side by side.

00:01:15.034 --> 00:01:18.382
Running your own validator means keeping the full

00:01:18.382 --> 00:01:21.864
reward for yourself, but it requires real technical

00:01:21.864 --> 00:01:26.114
setup, reliable uptime, and genuine ongoing responsibility.

00:01:26.114 --> 00:01:29.715
Delegating to someone else's validator is far simpler to

00:01:29.715 --> 00:01:32.559
start, but that validator takes a cut of the

00:01:32.559 --> 00:01:35.781
reward, and your outcome now depends partly on how

00:01:35.781 --> 00:01:37.929
well they run their own operation.

00:01:39.229 --> 00:01:41.847
Worth being precise about why the network pays

00:01:41.847 --> 00:01:43.852
for this at all, since it's easy to

00:01:43.852 --> 00:01:45.934
just treat the yield as a given.

00:01:45.934 --> 00:01:48.909
Proof-of-stake networks need a large amount of

00:01:48.909 --> 00:01:51.821
value genuinely at risk, specifically to make

00:01:51.821 --> 00:01:55.919
attacking or corrupting the chain economically unattractive.

00:01:55.919 --> 00:01:59.366
Staking rewards are the network's direct payment for taking

00:01:59.366 --> 00:02:01.836
on that role, and the risk that comes with

00:02:01.836 --> 00:02:04.479
it, not a reward for simply holding the token.

00:02:05.779 --> 00:02:08.918
Here's where that reward actually comes from, since the

00:02:08.918 --> 00:02:11.964
source matters for who ultimately bears its cost.

00:02:11.964 --> 00:02:16.070
Issuance means the network mints brand-new tokens specifically to

00:02:16.070 --> 00:02:20.164
pay stakers, which mildly dilutes everyone who isn't staking.

00:02:20.164 --> 00:02:23.291
Fees mean the reward comes from transaction fees

00:02:23.291 --> 00:02:27.311
actual users already paid, redistributed to whoever's securing

00:02:27.311 --> 00:02:30.419
the chain, with no dilution involved at all.

00:02:30.419 --> 00:02:33.454
Most networks blend both, in varying proportions.

00:02:34.754 --> 00:02:35.844
Quick check.

00:02:35.844 --> 00:02:38.399
What do staking rewards actually pay for?

00:02:42.819 --> 00:02:45.978
The answer: securing the network, specifically by

00:02:45.978 --> 00:02:48.189
putting real value at risk to make

00:02:48.189 --> 00:02:51.979
attacking or corrupting the chain economically unattractive.

00:02:53.279 --> 00:02:55.234
The real risks underneath the yield.

00:02:56.534 --> 00:02:58.354
Here's all three risks, and what

00:02:58.354 --> 00:03:00.309
each one actually does to you.

00:03:00.309 --> 00:03:03.378
Slashing is a direct penalty, on some networks,

00:03:03.378 --> 00:03:07.853
for validator misbehaviour, like double-signing or extended downtime,

00:03:07.853 --> 00:03:10.410
and it can cut directly into the staked

00:03:10.410 --> 00:03:13.139
principal itself, not just the reward.

00:03:13.139 --> 00:03:16.683
An unbonding period is a mandatory waiting time between

00:03:16.683 --> 00:03:20.733
requesting to withdraw and actually receiving your assets back,

00:03:20.733 --> 00:03:24.324
during which the market can move freely against you.

00:03:24.324 --> 00:03:27.516
And validator downtime simply means missed rewards,

00:03:27.516 --> 00:03:30.339
for however long that validator stays offline.

00:03:31.639 --> 00:03:34.385
Worth being precise about the unbonding period

00:03:34.385 --> 00:03:37.072
specifically, since it's the risk most likely

00:03:37.072 --> 00:03:38.949
to catch someone off guard.

00:03:38.949 --> 00:03:41.658
It isn't a fee, and it isn't optional; it's

00:03:41.658 --> 00:03:45.107
simply time you cannot access your assets, whatever the

00:03:45.107 --> 00:03:48.239
market happens to do during that exact window.

00:03:48.239 --> 00:03:52.154
On some networks that's days; on others, considerably longer.

00:03:52.154 --> 00:03:54.418
Know the actual number before you stake,

00:03:54.418 --> 00:03:56.074
not after you try to withdraw.

00:03:57.374 --> 00:04:00.845
Here's why delegating specifically changes your exposure to

00:04:00.845 --> 00:04:04.384
these risks, compared with running a validator yourself.

00:04:04.384 --> 00:04:07.818
Delegating to a validator with a strong uptime record

00:04:07.818 --> 00:04:11.760
and no history of slashing meaningfully lowers your practical

00:04:11.760 --> 00:04:14.604
exposure to both of the first two risks.

00:04:14.604 --> 00:04:18.188
Delegating to an unproven or poorly run validator can

00:04:18.188 --> 00:04:21.574
expose you to the exact same slashing and downtime

00:04:21.574 --> 00:04:25.224
risk as running one badly yourself, without you having

00:04:25.224 --> 00:04:27.614
any direct control over the outcome.

00:04:28.914 --> 00:04:31.543
Here's how to actually plan around an unbonding

00:04:31.543 --> 00:04:33.979
period, rather than being caught by it.

00:04:33.979 --> 00:04:36.381
Know the exact number of days before you ever

00:04:36.381 --> 00:04:38.979
stake, not when you suddenly need the funds.

00:04:38.979 --> 00:04:42.357
Start the withdrawal well ahead of whenever you actually

00:04:42.357 --> 00:04:45.971
expect to need that liquidity, treating the unbonding period

00:04:45.971 --> 00:04:48.345
as a hard floor, not a rough guess.

00:04:48.345 --> 00:04:51.346
And never stake capital you might need on short

00:04:51.346 --> 00:04:54.409
notice, since no urgency on your end changes how

00:04:54.409 --> 00:04:57.535
long the network's own waiting period actually is.

00:04:58.835 --> 00:04:59.925
Quick check.

00:04:59.925 --> 00:05:01.490
What exactly is unbonding?

00:05:05.910 --> 00:05:09.789
The answer: a mandatory waiting period, after requesting a

00:05:09.789 --> 00:05:14.720
withdrawal, before your unstaked assets actually become transferable again.

00:05:16.020 --> 00:05:17.025
Worked example.

00:05:18.325 --> 00:05:20.886
Here's the scenario, step by step, using

00:05:20.886 --> 00:05:24.810
the source material's own realistic, illustrative numbers.

00:05:24.810 --> 00:05:27.578
You stake an asset, earning roughly three percent

00:05:27.578 --> 00:05:29.650
a year, paid in that same asset.

00:05:29.650 --> 00:05:31.522
Over that same year, the asset's

00:05:31.522 --> 00:05:33.580
own price falls thirty percent.

00:05:33.580 --> 00:05:35.589
Your staked balance is genuinely

00:05:35.589 --> 00:05:37.715
larger, by that three percent.

00:05:37.715 --> 00:05:40.583
But its dollar value has fallen anyway, because

00:05:40.583 --> 00:05:43.750
the price move overwhelms the staking yield entirely.

00:05:45.050 --> 00:05:49.015
Here's the actual dollar math behind that, computed precisely.

00:05:49.015 --> 00:05:51.425
One point zero three, for the three

00:05:51.425 --> 00:05:54.572
percent staking gain, multiplied by zero point

00:05:54.572 --> 00:05:57.384
seven, for the thirty percent price drop,

00:05:57.384 --> 00:05:59.960
comes to zero point seven two one.

00:05:59.960 --> 00:06:02.884
That's a loss of about twenty-seven point nine

00:06:02.884 --> 00:06:06.307
percent, in dollar terms, despite every single staking

00:06:06.307 --> 00:06:08.920
reward being paid out exactly as promised.

00:06:10.220 --> 00:06:12.912
Here's the one sentence this entire worked

00:06:12.912 --> 00:06:15.666
example exists to prove, worth holding onto

00:06:15.666 --> 00:06:18.095
more than any specific percentage.

00:06:18.095 --> 00:06:20.689
Staking adds a small, genuine yield on top

00:06:20.689 --> 00:06:23.284
of whatever the asset does anyway; it does

00:06:23.284 --> 00:06:26.480
not protect you from that asset's price falling.

00:06:26.480 --> 00:06:30.225
The three percent helped, technically, exactly as advertised.

00:06:30.225 --> 00:06:32.675
It simply wasn't remotely close to being enough.

00:06:33.975 --> 00:06:36.860
The same 3%, across different price moves.

00:06:38.160 --> 00:06:41.612
Here's that same three percent staking yield, applied

00:06:41.612 --> 00:06:44.936
across five different price scenarios, from a sharp

00:06:44.936 --> 00:06:48.305
fall to a solid gain, again purely illustrative.

00:06:48.305 --> 00:06:50.609
At a fifty percent price fall, you're down

00:06:50.609 --> 00:06:53.855
about forty-eight and a half percent, staking included.

00:06:53.855 --> 00:06:55.736
At the thirty percent fall from a

00:06:55.736 --> 00:06:58.305
moment ago, down twenty-seven point nine.

00:06:58.305 --> 00:07:01.420
At a ten percent fall, down about seven percent.

00:07:01.420 --> 00:07:02.788
With the price flat, you're

00:07:02.788 --> 00:07:04.505
simply up your three percent.

00:07:04.505 --> 00:07:07.231
And with a thirty percent gain, staking adds a

00:07:07.231 --> 00:07:10.073
little more on top, to about thirty-four percent.

00:07:11.373 --> 00:07:14.013
Notice how little that three percent staking

00:07:14.013 --> 00:07:16.653
yield actually shifts any of these outcomes,

00:07:16.653 --> 00:07:18.948
compared to the price move itself.

00:07:18.948 --> 00:07:21.415
It nudges every single result by roughly

00:07:21.415 --> 00:07:24.243
three percentage points, in the same direction

00:07:24.243 --> 00:07:26.288
the price was already moving.

00:07:26.288 --> 00:07:29.895
It never once changes which direction the outcome goes.

00:07:29.895 --> 00:07:31.671
The price move decides that,

00:07:31.671 --> 00:07:33.080
on its own, every time.

00:07:34.379 --> 00:07:37.567
Here's what this actually implies for deciding what to

00:07:37.567 --> 00:07:40.292
stake in the first place, since the math above

00:07:40.292 --> 00:07:43.374
applies to any asset, not just this one example.

00:07:43.374 --> 00:07:46.084
An asset you'd genuinely hold regardless of

00:07:46.084 --> 00:07:48.917
its price, for reasons entirely separate from

00:07:48.917 --> 00:07:51.989
the staking yield, is a reasonable candidate.

00:07:51.989 --> 00:07:54.388
An asset you're only holding because of

00:07:54.388 --> 00:07:56.667
the staking rate itself is exposed to

00:07:56.667 --> 00:07:59.125
exactly the loss the worked example just

00:07:59.125 --> 00:08:01.584
showed you, the instant that price falls.

00:08:02.884 --> 00:08:03.974
Quick check.

00:08:03.974 --> 00:08:05.857
Does staking protect you against

00:08:05.857 --> 00:08:07.969
the underlying asset's price falling?

00:08:12.389 --> 00:08:14.469
The answer: no. It adds a small

00:08:14.469 --> 00:08:16.809
yield on top; the asset's own price

00:08:16.809 --> 00:08:19.864
move still dominates your actual dollar result.

00:08:21.164 --> 00:08:23.094
Checking a validator, concretely.

00:08:24.394 --> 00:08:26.885
Here's exactly what to check on a validator

00:08:26.885 --> 00:08:30.469
or delegation provider, before staking anything with them.

00:08:30.469 --> 00:08:33.863
Its uptime percentage, over as long a track record

00:08:33.863 --> 00:08:36.759
as you can find, not just recent weeks.

00:08:36.759 --> 00:08:38.633
Whether it's ever been slashed, and

00:08:38.633 --> 00:08:40.494
if so, what actually happened.

00:08:40.494 --> 00:08:43.774
Its fee, and how that compares to similar providers.

00:08:43.774 --> 00:08:46.796
And how concentrated total stake already is with

00:08:46.796 --> 00:08:49.817
it, since heavy concentration in one provider is

00:08:49.817 --> 00:08:52.099
its own separate risk to the network.

00:08:53.399 --> 00:08:54.859
Here's your checklist.

00:08:54.859 --> 00:08:56.744
Do it before staking anything.

00:08:56.744 --> 00:08:59.908
Choose your validator, or provider, based on its

00:08:59.908 --> 00:09:03.395
actual uptime record, its fee, how concentrated stake

00:09:03.395 --> 00:09:06.107
already is with it, and its track record,

00:09:06.107 --> 00:09:08.279
not just the advertised rate.

00:09:08.279 --> 00:09:10.086
Know the unbonding period, in

00:09:10.086 --> 00:09:12.254
actual days, before you commit.

00:09:12.254 --> 00:09:15.102
And stake only capital you'd genuinely hold

00:09:15.102 --> 00:09:18.274
anyway, regardless of the staking yield entirely.

00:09:19.574 --> 00:09:20.819
Let's recap.

00:09:20.819 --> 00:09:24.344
Staking rewards pay for securing the network, whether you

00:09:24.344 --> 00:09:27.319
run a validator yourself or delegate to one.

00:09:27.319 --> 00:09:30.228
The real risks are slashing, unbonding periods,

00:09:30.228 --> 00:09:32.834
and validator downtime, none of which show

00:09:32.834 --> 00:09:34.589
up in the headline rate.

00:09:34.589 --> 00:09:36.624
Staking adds a small yield on top

00:09:36.624 --> 00:09:38.899
of whatever the asset does anyway; it

00:09:38.899 --> 00:09:41.534
never protects against a falling price.

00:09:41.534 --> 00:09:43.577
And the rule that follows from all of

00:09:43.577 --> 00:09:46.104
it: stake what you'd hold anyway, nothing more.

00:09:47.404 --> 00:09:49.839
Choose your validator on its record, not

00:09:49.839 --> 00:09:52.392
just its rate, know your unbonding period,

00:09:52.392 --> 00:09:54.949
and only stake what you'd hold anyway.

00:09:54.949 --> 00:09:59.371
Next up, Lesson 4.3: liquid staking (LSTs).
